26. Key Takeaways
Initial Access

The initial foothold was obtained through an unauthenticated Langflow API endpoint.

The vulnerable endpoint accepted attacker-controlled flow definitions, ultimately allowing arbitrary Python code execution.

The important lesson is that publicly accessible workflow-building functionality should never execute arbitrary server-side code without strong isolation and authorization.
Credential Exposure

After obtaining the initial shell, sensitive credentials were exposed through environment variables:

LANGFLOW_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD
LANGFLOW_SECRET_KEY

This demonstrates why secrets should not be unnecessarily exposed to application processes or inherited into environments accessible by lower-privileged processes.
Internal Service Discovery

The .mcp/config.json configuration file disclosed credentials for an internal MCP service:

10.129.244.214:30080

The service was not directly exposed externally, but SSH tunneling made it accessible from the attacking machine.
JWT Misconfiguration

The MCP server advertised:

HS256
none

as supported JWT algorithms.

Accepting the none algorithm allowed the authentication token to be forged without a valid signature.

This converted a normal user token into an administrative token:

{
  "sub": "admin",
  "role": "admin"
}

MCP Tool Registration

Administrative access to the MCP API allowed arbitrary tools to be registered.

Because the tool implementation contained Python code executed by the server, this resulted in another RCE and a shell as the mcp user.
Kubernetes Privilege Escalation

The final stage involved abusing Kubernetes/kubelet access.

The combination of:

nodes/proxy

and access to the kubelet's:

:10250

exec functionality allowed commands to be executed in a pod with access to the host filesystem.

The host filesystem was available at:

/host/root

which ultimately allowed access to:

/host/root/root/root.txt

and completed the machine.
Conclusion

Fireflow demonstrates how multiple seemingly independent weaknesses can be chained together into a full compromise:

Web enumeration
    → Langflow RCE
    → credential disclosure
    → SSH access
    → internal service discovery
    → JWT misconfiguration
    → MCP RCE
    → Kubernetes abuse
    → host filesystem access
    → root flag

The most important lesson from this machine is that privilege escalation does not necessarily happen through a single traditional local exploit. In this case, the attack progressed through several trust boundaries:

Web application → Linux user → internal API → authentication layer → MCP execution → Kubernetes → host.
